      "message": "To note down my thoughts somewhere before I disappear next week.\n---\n\nI thought about this again, AFAICT, there are about 3 (more or less feasible) options:\n\na) The option presented in this patch -- NetworkAgent callbacks run on a separate thread and wait for the network to be destroyed during stop. The benefit of this solution is that stop() will block the main handler thread while all this happens. I think this is the only solution that lets us get away with the current 2 state model (started vs. stopped).\n\nb) We add a StoppingState that waits for the network destroyed callback which can now happen on the main handler thread (similar to IpClient). This is hard to do without a proper StateMachine and even then it would add additional complexity (properly handling all handler callbacks while in this state).\n\nc) (this will likely not even work and is a terrible idea): We stop IpClient from within the NetworkAgent#unwanted() callback. This means stop would call NetworkAgent#unregister(), which would eventually trigger an unwanted() callback which then cleans up everything else. If this were to work, there is a huge potential for side effects that I do not clearly understand at this point. This feels very risky.",
